1c6295" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479137}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power of Product Rule to Expressions with Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "824f067a-aafa-5691-a971-7a05644aa56b" :step/math-academy-id 17141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130340 :tutorial/content "To raise the product of two numbers to a power, we can raise each number to that power, and then multiply. This is the **power of product rule for exponents**. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $(5 \\times 3)^4$ by applying the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n(5\\times 3)^4=5^4\\times 3^4\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power of product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5\\times 3)^{4} & = \\\\ {{(5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {{(5\\times 5\\times 5\\times 5)}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}}\\times {{(3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3)}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {5}^{4}\\times {3}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79b0e407-5948-563e-a8af-5dbcd3ab59f3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3272 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "308748b1-5bd7-5b3a-94ef-bd3c9df25cfe" :step/math-academy-id 17996 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129935}}] :activity/title "The Power of Product Rule for Exponents" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129355}]}
